---
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';

import catalog from '../../../generated/errors.json';

const errors = Object.entries(catalog).toSorted(([, a], [, b]) => a.status - b.status);
---
<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title: 'API errors',
    description: 'Understand Backbricks error responses and find each error by code.',
  }}
>
  <p>
    The JSON API describes failures as <code>application/problem+json</code>. Branch on <code>code</code>
    in your integration. Read <code>detail</code> and <code>errors</code> when present, and include
    <code>requestId</code> when asking a deployment operator for help.
  </p>
  <h2 id="common-failures">Common failures</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      For validation failures, use <code>errors[].path</code> to find the field and
      <code>errors[].code</code> to choose a message. If a plain HTML form reports an error on
      <code>key</code>, copy the current action URL from <strong>Embed</strong> and check that the
      project has an active publishable key.
    </li>
    <li>
      If a request is denied, check the website's exact origin for browser requests or the key's
      scopes for server requests. Rotate a key reported as exposed and remove it from website code.
    </li>
    <li>For rate limits, wait for the time in <code>Retry-After</code> before trying again.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The SDK exposes <code>code</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>fieldErrors</code>, and
    <code>requestId</code> on <code>BackbricksError</code>.
  </p>
  <h2 id="error-catalog">Error catalog</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Status</th>
        <th scope="col">Code</th>
        <th scope="col">Meaning</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {errors.map(([code, { status, title }]) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{status}</td>
          <td><a href={`/docs/errors/${code}`}><code>{code}</code></a></td>
          <td>{title}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</StarlightPage>
